WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.399
General chat models provide broad assistance.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:06.344
Coding models emphasize software tasks.

00:00:06.344 --> 00:00:11.103
Reasoning-oriented models may spend more time on complex problems.

00:00:11.103 --> 00:00:15.408
Embedding models convert text into vectors for retrieval.

00:00:15.408 --> 00:00:19.713
Vision-language models process images as well as text.

00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:28.097
Select models by task tests, language needs, context, license, provenance, runtime support, memory, latency, and error behavior.

00:00:28.097 --> 00:00:35.121
A larger model may improve difficult work while making ordinary interaction slow or expensive.

00:00:35.121 --> 00:00:40.333
Quantization reduces numerical precision to lower memory and compute requirements.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:44.185
Quality tradeoffs vary by model and task.

00:00:44.185 --> 00:00:52.115
Compare several practical quantizations instead of assuming the highest precision is always the best system choice.

00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:56.421
Ollama and LM Studio simplify common local workflows.

00:00:56.421 --> 00:00:57.553
llama.

00:00:57.553 --> 00:01:01.406
cpp provides lower-level control for compatible models.

00:01:01.406 --> 00:01:07.523
Open WebUI offers a web interface that can connect to supported services.

00:01:07.523 --> 00:01:11.829
API-compatible servers help applications integrate with local inference.

00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:18.853
Runtime choice affects model formats, drivers, acceleration, context, batching, concurrency, logging, APIs, and security.

00:01:18.853 --> 00:01:28.143
A model that works in one tool may behave differently in another because prompt templates and generation defaults change.

00:01:28.143 --> 00:01:33.354
Shortlist a few complete configurations and evaluate them before committing.

00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:39.472
Record model, file, quantization, runtime, backend, prompt template, settings, hardware, and date.

00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:44.231
Treat model upgrades as application changes, not transparent replacements.

00:01:44.231 --> 00:01:56.240
A model-routing or fallback policy can assign simple work to a small local model and escalate difficult requests to an approved stronger service or human.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.545
Routing must honor data classification and user expectations.
